A||N||4||maffthekat||Paint your wagon||||5||
Z||000000||maffthekat||04-05-2001||10:34 AM||||Hi,<BR>I bought a red '84 notchback a few months ago with 87K on the clock and a dodgy paint job from the previous owner (bits of red paint on the black stripe and bumpers etc).<P>Naturally I want to respray my beloved beast and restore it to its original showroom shine.<P>I've hear the best way to do this is to unbolt all the panels and get them prepped and resprayed individually.<P>Is it easy to do this? Is it easy to get the black stripe off without breaking the clips?<BR>Can you give me some tips on how to do this.<P>Also does anyone have any recommendations on what shade / colour red paint to use which will look cool?<P>Cheers<P>Matt||||reg||5
Z||000001||Wade87GT||04-05-2001||10:53 AM||||Welcome to the Forum. About how many Fieros are over there? I don't have the answer to your question, but here's a thread that might help: <A HREF="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/Forum2/HTML/008882.html" TARGET=_blank>http://www.fiero.nl/forum/Forum2/HTML/008882.html</A> <P>Wade||||reg||1
Z||000002||maffthekat||04-05-2001||11:08 AM||||Hi Wade,<P>Fiero's seem to be fairly rare over here - I've looked in the UK Auto Trader on the web which is the main boy, and I've only ever seen two on there. There are some American car clubs, but not thier all Cadillacs, Buicks and Trans Ams... I get all my Fiero parts from the Fiero Store at the mo.<P>I'm thinking of starting a UK Fiero site, I'll see how things go...<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000003||Pontiaddict||04-05-2001||08:18 PM||||If you want to paint it red. (not my first choice but you're not me so...) I'd go for a dark metallic red. <P>Painting all of the parts you want that color at the same time reduces the chance of having variances in color of the different panels.||||reg||1
Z||000004||MrPBody||04-05-2001||09:23 PM||||You <I>will</I> break the clips.  Fiero Store sells replacements.<P>Based on advice from other Forum members who paint cars more often than I: you'll get best results by removing some panels to paint concealed edges, jambs, <I>etc.</I>, then reinstalling panels to paint the entire exterior in one pass.  If you paint panels individually -- even with the same batch of paint -- colors may not match.<P>Good luck!<BR>||||reg||1
